{
  "id": "COMPOUND:35115 - PROTEIN:6326",
  "verbalization": "Iristectorigenin A appears to exert its biological effects through a multi-step mechanism involving DNA polymerase nu, which is a target of two different compounds—Griffithinam and Theonellasterol B. Both of these compounds are predicted to interact with DNA polymerase nu, which in turn is a known target of Phosphodiesterase 6C. This suggests that Iristectorigenin A may act through a similar pathway, potentially modulating DNA polymerase nu activity, which could influence downstream processes. The presence of a third compound, (4S,5S,5aS,9aS)-4-chloro-5-hydroxy-6,6,9a-trimethyl-4,5,5a,7,8,9-hexahydro-1H-benzo[e][2]benzofuran-3-one, further supports the notion that Iristectorigenin A may interact with DNA polymerase nu and ultimately affect Phosphodiesterase 6C. The integrated evidence suggests a plausible mechanistic link, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated. The observed scores indicate a consistent pattern of interaction, but the precise molecular mechanism and the role of each intermediate compound require further investigation.",
